      "sourceTextSha256": "ada9003c574b71c4664c536d0021c5dad82a0599c8ddf660d60a0c621beaf6d6",
      "text": "Get up at 05:30 to watch the sunrise over the salt flats.\n\nThe Uyuni Salt Flat lies on a high plateau at about 3,650 m above sea level and covers roughly 11,000 square kilometres. The salt layer is up to 10 m thick in the centre. In the dry season, the surface looks like a vast white desert. In the rainy season, a thin layer of water turns it into a giant mirror.\n\nOn the way, you'll visit Colchani, where you can see how salt is extracted and processed. The next stop is Ojos de Agua, or Eyes of Water, where you'll see natural geological formations and places where water rises to the surface.\n\nContinue to Incahuasi Island. Giant cacti grow here, and the higher ground offers views across the salt flats. Admission to the island is 30 bolivianos per person, payable separately.\n\nArrive in Uyuni at around 18:00. If you choose the itinerary that ends in Chile, a transfer to San Pedro de Atacama is arranged.",
